faqs=[
('ING-FAQ-01','What heavy metals were measured in applesauce?','FDA measured lead, cadmium, total arsenic, total mercury, nickel, chromium and uranium in the two applesauce categories shown here. All were non-detects in the three bottled composites; two of eight baby-food composites had nickel detections, with the remaining endpoints non-detects.',[fda],'Original FDA rows 15324–15384 and 31662–31827'),
('ING-FAQ-02','How did lead get into the recalled cinnamon applesauce?','Investigators traced the lead source to the cinnamon ingredient. This outbreak finding does not identify the apple base as the source or establish the responsible actor, addition point or motive.',[nap,tro],'Napier Nationwide Investigation; Troeschel Introduction/Discussion'),
('ING-FAQ-03','Is organic applesauce free of heavy metals?','These applesauce records do not establish an organic-versus-conventional comparison. FDA’s food-list categories do not provide the production-system information needed to rank those labels; a product label cannot replace a scoped analytical result.',[fda],'FDA food definitions and result fields'),
('ING-FAQ-04','Was bottled applesauce lower than baby-food applesauce?','Two baby-food composites measured 50 and 86 µg/kg nickel, above the <40 µg/kg bound for all three bottled composites, but six baby-food composites were also <40 µg/kg. The unmatched sampling frames and censored results cannot establish a general advantage for either form.',[fda],'FDA Ni rows; Variation'),
('ING-FAQ-05','Which apple-growing region has the lowest metal levels?','No orchard-origin ranking can be recovered from these records. FDA collection regions describe where food was collected, while orchard, cultivar and origin-linked finished-lot results are unavailable for a comparable ranking.',[fda],'FDA key Region and FoodDescription'),
('ING-FAQ-06','Can an added ingredient change the result for an applesauce pouch?','Yes: the investigation identified cinnamon as the lead source in the implicated puree. A result for plain applesauce therefore cannot clear a cinnamon-containing or other blended formulation.',[nap,tro],'Napier Nationwide Investigation'),
('ING-FAQ-07','Do the FDA non-detects prove applesauce is safe?','No. They bound the concentrations in the tested composites at the stated reporting limits; they do not establish every current lot’s concentration or an individual exposure assessment.',[fda],'Original result/reporting-limit fields; Testing'),
('ING-FAQ-08','Are there legal limits for metals in applesauce?','Current legal applicability has not been verified for the exact applesauce form, intended use and sales market in this review. Fresh-fruit limits and baby-food guidance require separate primary-instrument checks before they can be applied to a finished puree.',[],'Assessment; precise current-law verification boundary'),
('ING-FAQ-09','Has peeling or cooking been shown to lower metals in applesauce?','The FDA dataset does not pair an apple input with a finished applesauce output, so it cannot quantify a peeling, washing or cooking effect. No percentage reduction or validated household removal treatment is established by the selected records.',[fda],'Prepared-food study design; Processing'),
('ING-FAQ-10','Does a total arsenic or chromium result identify its chemical form?','No. The applesauce endpoints are total arsenic and total chromium; they do not supply inorganic arsenic or hexavalent chromium concentrations. The chromium finding in the outbreak ingredient does not speciate every routine applesauce sample.',[fda,tro],'FDA analyte fields; Troeschel Discussion'),
('extra-nd','Does a reported zero mean no metal was present?','No. The zero-coded FDA applesauce results are interpreted as non-detects, with separate reporting limits retained. For example, bottled-applesauce lead is <4 µg/kg rather than a measured zero.',[fda],'FDA applesauce Pb concentration/reporting-limit rows'),
('extra-units','Are ppb and µg/kg different concentrations?','For these mass-basis food results, 1 ppb equals 1 µg/kg. That unit equivalence does not convert fresh apple, dried apple or juice into the same material as ready-to-eat puree.',[fda],'FDA key units; no basis conversion'),
('ING-FAQ-13','Why is the cinnamon-pouch outbreak relevant to children?','The reports linked contaminated cinnamon-containing puree to childhood lead exposure and describe public-health investigation and follow-up. They concern implicated products and do not provide a safe weekly allowance for ordinary applesauce.',[nap,tro],'Both abstracts and Discussion'),
('extra-count','Were there 566 contaminated food samples?','No. The national follow-up reported 566 cases of lead poisoning after consumption of recalled products. These are case-defined people, distinct from food samples, brands, lots or independent outbreaks.',[tro],'Troeschel Abstract and Case Ascertainment'),
('extra-test','What should an applesauce test report identify?','It should identify the sampled lot and recipe, measured elements or species, units, prepared-food basis, method and reporting limits. This is a proposed interpretation of the source measurement requirements; a composite or historical survey result does not test every unit or added ingredient.',[fda,nap],'FDA key and Napier product tracing; Testing'),
('extra-recall','What control did the recall investigation actually document?','Authorities issued warnings and a recall, then checked for remaining implicated stock and removed products found during store visits. These are documented exposure-prevention actions; the report does not quantify a metal-removal percentage from puree.',[nap],'Initial Response; North Carolina Recall Audit'),
('ING-FAQ-16','Can these results tell me a safe number of servings per week?','No universal safe serving frequency follows from the selected measurements. A serving assessment needs the actual formulation, concentrations, intake pattern and relevant population; the outbreak reports do not supply an allowance for plain applesauce.',[fda,nap,tro],'Sampling scope and outbreak population; Exposure')]
